 ## Background
-Isotropic Residuum Propagation was introduced in the paper "Principal-Ordinates Propagation for Real-Time Rendering of Participating Media" by Elek et al. (2014) [S1]. It is part of the Principal-Ordinates Propagation method, which aims to solve false scattering and ray effects in finite-element light transport methods. The technique leverages the property that scattering reduces radiance anisotropy, allowing a switch to cheaper isotropic propagation in later stages [S1].
+Isotropic Residuum Propagation was introduced in the paper "Principal-Ordinates Propagation for Real-Time Rendering of Participating Media" by Elek et al. (2014) [S1]. It is part of the Principal-Ordinates Propagation method, which aims to solve false scattering and ray effects in finite-element light transport methods [S1, G]. The technique leverages the property that scattering reduces radiance anisotropy, allowing a switch to cheaper isotropic propagation in later stages [S1].
 
